<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Legal codification</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Legal codification is the recurring process by which rulers gathered, standardised and proclaimed laws, strengthening central authority and the uniform administration of justice. Codes such as the Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> in the Babylonian Empire, Justinian's codification in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasa">Yassa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and Ottoman kanun law gave states a basis of legitimacy and order. Codification often accompanied periods of consolidation and left lasting legal legacies.</p></div>
